WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:30.770
<v Speaker 1>The 2004 Magic the Gathering World Championships are over. History was made today as rookie Julian Naughton became the new world champion. At 15 years old, Julian is the youngest world champion in Magic history. He also broke the records by taking home $52,300 this weekend. This is the largest play out for a trading card game ever. The team finals draft between Belgium and Germany was a close one, with both teams claiming that they had the advantage after the draft.</v>

00:00:31.010 --> 00:01:00.900
<v Speaker 1>In the end, it came down to rest on the shoulders of the Champions, with both teams winning a match. It came to an exciting game 3. Belgian champ Lemoyne started pounding down on the German champ, making only one subtle error, missing a single point of damage. But that error came back to haunt him as TOEFL turned the game around from a 20 to one life disadvantage to win the title. When the best of the best battle, even one point can mean the difference between being a finalist and being a champion. The individual quarterfinals</v>

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:32.010
<v Speaker 1>we're all about upsets, as fan favorite Camille Cornelsen lost Emmanuel Bovan, whose Iron Work combo deck made 30 mere tokens on the third turn in two of the three games he won to take the match. Gabriel Nassif was taken down by a rookie in the top 8 AO Pacquette in an exciting game five. Don't feel too badly for him though, as Nasif earned Magic's highest honor this weekend, being named the 2004 Player of the Year. In the other quarterfinals match, Julian Naughton crushed Murray the Muller Evans</v>

00:01:32.110 --> 00:02:03.500
<v Speaker 1>and his unusual anti control affinity deck with Juliano's green white slide deck. Japanese player Rio Ogura beat the odds and defeated Malaysian Terry SOS anti aggressive slide deck with a goblin beat down. Rios luck didn't hold up in the semis however, as he was taken down by Julian slide control strategy. In the other semifinal match, Manual Blevans moment in the spotlight ended when his controversial side boarding strategy of taking out his entire Ironworks combo failed to work against Paquette. At the end of five days of grueling</v>

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:33.510
<v Speaker 1>competition, only two remained. The final round was an exciting battle, but these young rookies impressed everyone with the speed and skill displayed today. In only 30 minutes, Julian took down AO in a three to one victory and was crowned as the World Champion. Congratulations to Julia Noughton, world champion, to the German World Championship team and to Gabriel Nassif, player of the year. Be sure to join us for all the exciting action in Columbus as we kick off</v>

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.320
<v Speaker 1>the 2005 season.</v>

